# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica> ,
              <https://www.theoi.com/> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Finding the right Wikidata property. The brief suggested P2959, which is \"permanent duplicated item\" and does not link gods. The statements of Zeus (Q34201) were listed (query 1); the Greek–Roman correspondences are recorded with P460 \"said to be the same as\" (Zeus → Jupiter Q4649) and the god's domains with P2925 \"domain of saint or deity\"."@en ,
                 "Candidates. The twelve Olympians (Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, Demeter, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Ares, Aphrodite, Hephaestus, Hermes, and both Hestia and Dionysus, since lists of the twelve differ on these two), plus Hades, Persephone, Eros and Cronus, and the personified gods Helios, Selene, Eos, Nike and Tyche, whose Roman counterparts are one-to-one. Their P460 values with English and Swedish labels were fetched with query 2; Eros, Helios, Nike and Dionysus were looked up by their English Wikipedia sitelinks (API call 4), and the labels, aliases and Swedish sitelinks of all Greek and Roman items with API call 5."@en ,
                 "Choosing the counterpart. P460 often lists several equivalents (Zeus: Jupiter, Thor, Amun, Indra and others). For each god the Roman deity among the P460 values was taken; where two Roman gods were listed (Hades: Pluto and Dis Pater; Dionysus: Bacchus and Liber; Persephone: Proserpina and Libera; Selene: Luna and Diana; Eros: Cupid via Q5011), the choice was made from the content sources and the other is mentioned in a note where it is common: Pluto, whose Wikidata description is \"god in Roman religion, Plouton in Greek\" and of whom the 1911 Britannica (\"Pluto\") says that among the Romans Hades \"was usually called Dis pater ... and Orcus, although the name Pluto is often used\" (so the card states this convention in its note); Bacchus, \"ancient Roman deity of wine\" in Wikidata, which the 1911 Britannica (\"Liber and Libera\") gives as another name of Liber; Proserpina, \"the Latin form of Persephone\" (1911 Britannica, \"Proserpine\"), Libera being only \"sometimes identified\" with her; Luna, \"Roman goddess of the Moon\" in Wikidata, Diana being Artemis's counterpart; Cupid, \"Roman deity, counterpart of Eros\" in Wikidata and \"the Latin name for the god of love, Eros\" in the 1911 Britannica (\"Cupid\"). The Theoi title lines agree with every choice but did not decide any (as first drafted, this step used them as the tie-break; changed in review round 3)."@en ,
                 "Names on the cards. English names are the Wikidata English labels (Cronus, Hephaestus, Mercury, Vulcan, Neptune, Saturn, Cupid). Swedish names are the Wikidata Swedish labels (Afrodite, Hefaistos, Persefone, Kronos, Apollon, Mercurius, Vulcanus, Neptunus, Saturnus, Amor), except where Wikidata had none: Dionysus (Q41680) has no Swedish label (only the misspelt alias \"Dinosys\"), so \"Dionysos\" was taken from the item's Swedish Wikipedia sitelink title recorded in Wikidata (API call 5, CC0) and confirmed in the article itself; Sol (Q1199937) has the Swedish label \"Sol Indiges\" and the alias \"Sol\", and \"Sol\" was used. Each Swedish name was confirmed against the Swedish Wikipedia article (API calls 9–11)."@en ,
                 "Domains. The domains of each god were taken from Wikidata (P2925 values with their English and Swedish labels, queries 3a and 3b, and the items' English descriptions) and written as a short phrase in our own words in English and Swedish, keeping only the god's main, uncontested domains. Apollo has no P2925 statement and is typed only as a \"solar deity\", a late identification; his domains (music, prophecy and healing) were taken from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica article (fetched from Wikisource, API call 7). Persephone has no P2925 statement and Wikidata describes her only as goddess of the seasons and queen of the underworld; her answer (the underworld and spring vegetation) follows the 1911 Britannica article \"Proserpine\" (query 14). The Dionysus answer is \"wine\" (Wikidata P2925), with his vegetation aspect and ecstatic worship from the 1911 Britannica article in a note; the Tyche answer is \"fortune\", Wikidata's description (both shortened in review rounds 1 and 3, see there). Swedish answers name the domains in the definite form (\"kriget\", \"kärleken och skönheten\"), as Swedish names a god's domain with a definite genitive (\"krigets gud\"). No domain card was written for Cronus (see selection)."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every card was compared with the god's Theoi Project page (fetched with query 6: the title line \"… (Roman X)\" and the first paragraph \"X was the god of …\"), with the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica articles (query 7 for the Olympians, query 14 for Pluto, Proserpine, Cupid, Liber and Libera and Fortuna) and with the first paragraph of the Swedish Wikipedia article (API calls 9–11). All sources agree on every correspondence and domain on the cards, except that the 1911 Britannica's article Dionysus names no Roman counterpart (its article \"Liber and Libera\" does) and that it says the Romans usually called Hades Dis pater or Orcus (see the note on hades-roman); differences in emphasis are recorded in the quality-control log."@en ,
                 "The dossier's Wikidata checks (English and Swedish labels of every Greek and Roman god on the cards, the P460 link for every correspondence and P2925 values for the domains) were run by the builder against live Wikidata on every build."@en ,
                 "Selection: The deck covers the twelve Olympians (counting both Hestia and Dionysus, since ancient lists differ on which of the two belongs to the twelve), Hades, Persephone, Eros and Cronus, and five personified gods with a single standard Roman counterpart: Helios (Sol), Selene (Luna), Eos (Aurora), Nike (Victoria) and Tyche (Fortuna). Each god has a card asking for the Roman counterpart; each except Cronus has a card asking for the god's domains. Left out: gods with no single standard Roman counterpart (Gaia: Terra or Tellus; Hecate; Pan: Faunus is only partly equivalent; Uranus; Rhea: Ops or Cybele) and minor gods; the domain card for Cronus, because the common description of him as god of time comes from an ancient confusion of Kronos with Chronos, and he is better described as king of the Titans than as a god of something. Domains are limited to each god's main, generally agreed ones; later or contested identifications (Apollo as sun god, Artemis as moon goddess) are left out of the answers and mentioned in a note where helpful."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose licences allow anything: Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0, gave the correspondences, the English and Swedish names and most domains, and the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, in the public domain (published 1910–1911, before 1931, in the US; elsewhere its articles used are anonymous or by contributors who died more than 70 years ago, see the source), gave the domains of Apollo and Persephone, the note on Dionysus and confirmation of the Roman names; facts and names are not copyrightable in any case, and the domain phrases are our own wording. The Theoi Project (all rights reserved) and Swedish Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) were used only to verify facts: every answer and note rests on Wikidata or the 1911 Britannica, no prose was copied from them (the Athena answer \"wisdom, war and crafts\" coincides with a Theoi title line, but is a plain list of three domains all given by Wikidata), and the selection of gods is our own (the Olympians and the gods named in the editor's brief, plus five personified gods with a one-to-one Roman counterpart), not taken from any list on those sites. The modern Encyclopædia Britannica was not consulted (britannica.com refused the request) and nothing was taken from it."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks against Wikidata and cross-checks of every card against the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, the Theoi Project and Swedish Wikipedia"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 43 cards, the property used for the correspondences, the Swedish names and the deck's licensing."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The builder's Wikidata checks cover the English and Swedish labels or aliases of every Greek and Roman god named on the cards (except the Swedish name Dionysos, which Wikidata lacks), the P460 link from each Greek god to the Roman counterpart on the card, and P2925 values for the domains where Wikidata has them. Every correspondence agrees across Wikidata, Theoi and Swedish Wikipedia (and the 1911 Britannica for the Olympians). The discrepancies found and how they were resolved are listed below."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 43 cards, re-checked against Wikidata (P2925 of every god, the 136 builder checks), the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica (Dionysus, Proserpine, Pluto, Demeter, Artemis), six Theoi Project pages and Swedish Wikipedia."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No card was found wrong; all 22 correspondences and the Swedish names are the standard ones. Three warnings (the Pluto convention, and \"spring growth\" and \"festivity\" resting only on verification sources) and two suggestions (the god count in the description, a Hestia check). All five were verified against the sources and fixed."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language and translation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 43 cards in English and Swedish, plus the title, description and keywords."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No Swedish name was found wrong and the language tags are right. One warning (definite and indefinite forms mixed in the Swedish domain answers) and five suggestions. Five were applied; the \"Dis pater\" spelling was declined."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 43 cards' evidence (every quoted \"says\" string searched in re-fetched sources), the four sources' licences, the attribution in the deck and the report, and the method, queries and licensing text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The licences and attribution were found correct and every quote was found in its source. The problems were places where verification-only sources (Theoi, Swedish Wikipedia) had supplied wording or choices. All nine findings were verified and fixed (the athena-domain part of one needed only a documentation change). After this round every answer and note rests on Wikidata or the 1911 Britannica."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final re-check (loop 1): changed cards, every third card (facts and language), metadata, licence and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: 29 of the 43 cards (the 18 changed in round 3 and every third card), the metadata, the licence and the documentation; builder check and validate_sources.ts re-run."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; one suggestion, on eros-domain, which was verified and applied by shortening the answer to what the content sources state."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica>
    dcterms:title "Encyclopædia Britannica, 11th edition (1911), as transcribed on Wikisource" ;
    dcterms:creator "Hugh Chisholm (editor) and the Encyclopædia Britannica's contributors; transcription by Wikisource contributors" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica — licence notice on the page: \"This work is in the public domain in the United States because it was published before January 1, 1931. This work may be in the public domain in countries and areas with longer native copyright terms that apply the rule of the shorter term to foreign works.\" Published 1910–1911. Outside the US: the articles used are either unsigned (Poseidon, Ares, Hestia, Dionysus, Pluto, Cupid, Eros (god), Liber and Libera, and the anonymous part of Proserpine), so anonymous works published over 70 years ago, or by contributors who died more than 70 years ago (Zeus: Lewis Richard Farnell, d. 1934; Hera, Hephaestus, Hermes: Edward Ernest Sikes, d. 1940; Demeter, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Aphrodite: John Henry Freese, d. 1930; Proserpine: James George Frazer, d. 1941; Fortuna: William Warde Fowler, d. 1921; signatures from the Wikisource headers and the articles' initials, death dates from Wikidata, query 15), and only facts, no text, were taken from them."@en .

<https://www.theoi.com/>
    dcterms:title "Theoi Project: Greek Mythology" ;
    dcterms:creator "Aaron J. Atsma" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Zeus.html — page footer: \"Theoi Project © Copyright 2000 - 2017 Aaron J. Atsma, Netherlands & New Zealand\". No open licence is offered, so the site is used for verification only."@en .

<https://sv.wikipedia.org/>
